Q: ASP.NET Core Web API Swagger always returning 401 A: <p>The reason Postman works while Swagger fails is a mismatch in your Swagger <code>OpenApiSecurityScheme</code> configuration: <strong><code>Name</code> property setting and duplicate <code>&quot;Bearer &quot;</code> prefixes.</strong></p>
<h5>The 2 Main Bugs in Your Swagger Setup</h5>
<ol>
<li><p><strong>Incorrect <code>Name</code> Header Value:</strong> You set <code>Name = &quot;Auth&quot;</code>. The HTTP header name for bearer authentication must be <code>Authorization</code>, not <code>Auth</code>. Because of this, Swagger is sending your token in an <code>Auth: Bearer &lt;token&gt;</code> header, which ASP.NET Core's JWT middleware ignores.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Double &quot;Bearer&quot; Prefix:</strong> When you configure <code>Scheme = &quot;Bearer&quot;</code> and <code>Type = SecuritySchemeType.Http</code> (or standard ApiKey mapping), Swagger automatically prepends <code>&quot;Bearer &quot;</code> to the value you paste into the UI popup. If you manually type <code>&quot;Bearer &lt;token&gt;&quot;</code> in the input box, Swagger sends <code>Authorization: Bearer Bearer &lt;token&gt;</code>, causing validation to fail.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Solution</strong></p>
<ol>
<li>Update <code>Program.cs</code> Swagger Setup
Use <code>SecuritySchemeType.Http</code> with <code>Scheme = &quot;bearer&quot;</code>. This is the standard, cleaner OpenAPI definition for JWT Bearer schemes:</li>
</ol>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>builder.Services.AddSwaggerGen(options =&gt;
{
    options.AddSecurityDefinition(&quot;Bearer&quot;, new OpenApiSecurityScheme
    {
        In = ParameterLocation.Header,
        Description = &quot;Please enter token&quot;,
        Name = &quot;Authorization&quot;, // Must be 'Authorization'
        Type = SecuritySchemeType.Http, // Standard HTTP Auth scheme
        BearerFormat = &quot;JWT&quot;,
        Scheme = &quot;bearer&quot; // Prepends 'Bearer ' automatically
    });

    options.AddSecurityRequirement(new OpenApiSecurityRequirement
    {
        {
            new OpenApiSecurityScheme
            {
                Reference = new OpenApiReference
                {
                    Type = ReferenceType.SecurityScheme,
                    Id = &quot;Bearer&quot;
                }
            },
            Array.Empty&lt;string&gt;()
        }
    });
});
</code></pre>
<ol start="2">
<li>How to Use in Swagger UI</li>
</ol>
<ul>
<li>Click the Authorize button in Swagger UI.</li>
<li>Paste ONLY the raw JWT token (do NOT type <code>Bearer</code> before it).</li>
<li>Click Authorize and test your endpoint.</li>
</ul>

Q: Why this code is not converting binary to decimal A: <p>Your algorithm is correct for manipulating the bits of an integer, but it is not converting the user's input from binary to decimal.</p>
<p>When you do:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>long long n;
cin &gt;&gt; n;
</code></pre>
<p>and the user enters 101, std::cin stores the <strong>decimal value</strong> 101, not the binary value 0b101.</p>
<p>Therefore, the expressions:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>n &amp; 1;
n &gt;&gt;= 1;
</code></pre>
<p>operate on the binary representation of <strong>101 decimal</strong> (1100101₂), not on the digits the user typed. As a result, your loop reconstructs the original decimal value instead of converting &quot;101&quot; to 5.</p>
<p>If the input is meant to be a binary number entered as 101, you should process the input <strong>digit by digit</strong> (or read it as a std::string and convert each character '0'/'1'). Bitwise operators (&amp;, &gt;&gt;) only manipulate the binary representation of an integer that has already been parsed; they do not convert a sequence of decimal characters into a binary value.</p>

Q: Frameless windows with qt5 (qml) A: <p>This for example, works on Ubuntu:</p>
<pre><code>import QtQuick 2.2
import QtQuick.Window 2.0
import QtQuick.Controls 1.1
import QtQuick.Controls.Styles 1.1

ApplicationWindow {
    id: backlight
    flags: Qt.FramelessWindowHint
    visible: true
    title: qsTr(&quot;backlight&quot;)
    width: 500
    height: 50
    x: (Screen.width - width) / 2
    y: (Screen.height - height) / 2
    color: &quot;transparent&quot;

    property real slideValue
    signal onSlide(real value)

    Rectangle {
        anchors.centerIn: parent
        width: parent.width
        height: 50
        color: &quot;transparent&quot;

        Rectangle {
            anchors.fill: parent
            radius: 25
            opacity: 0.3
            color: &quot;gray&quot;
        }

        Slider {
            anchors.centerIn: parent
            width: backlight.width - 16
            height: backlight.height
            value: backlight.slideValue
            focus: true
            onValueChanged: backlight.onSlide(value)
            Keys.onSpacePressed: Qt.quit()
            Keys.onEscapePressed: Qt.quit()

            style: SliderStyle {
                groove: Rectangle {
                    implicitHeight: 8
                    radius: 4
                    color: &quot;gray&quot;
                }
                handle: Rectangle {
                    anchors.centerIn: parent
                    color: control.pressed ? &quot;white&quot; : &quot;lightgray&quot;
                    border.color: &quot;gray&quot;
                    border.width: 2
                    width: 34
                    height: 34
                    radius: 17
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>It's an snippet from this project: <a href="https://github.com/oblitum/backlight/tree/cpp" rel="noreferrer">https://github.com/oblitum/backlight/tree/cpp</a></p>
<p>Initially I was using <a href="https://github.com/oblitum/backlight/commit/d6d99bebc3462b4bca7173b3902d9df84e1999b0" rel="noreferrer"><code>Qt.SplashScreen | Qt.FramelessWindowHint</code></a> but it didn't make a difference for me on Ubuntu (others also use <code>Qt.SubWindow | Qt.Tool | Qt.FramelessWindowHint | Qt.WindowSystemMenuHint</code>), so I left only <code>Qt.FramelessWindowHint</code>. It may make a difference for you.</p>
<p>This is Qt 5.2.</p>
<h2>EDIT</h2>
<p>Nevermind, <code>Qt.SplashScreen</code> did make a difference: no application icon is created.</p>
